}

\Babstract{
We propose the ability to disable interactions with \tt{errno} in mathematical functions.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

Functions in header \header{cmath} currently write to \tt{errno} to indicate errors such
as domain errors, overflow, or underflow.
For example, \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} doesn't simply yield NaN;
it also sets \tt{errno} to \tt{EDOM}.

\h3{Performance problem}

The runtime checks and write to \tcode{errno}
can worsen the performance of mathematical operations
and disable several optimizations because mathematical functions are not pure.
\tcode{std::sqrt} is an extreme example
because the entire operation can be performed within a few CPU cycles
(approx. 20 cycles latency, 6 cycles throughput)
and using a single CPU instruction:

\style{
  .center {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .tony-table td {
    background-color: var(--deep-background-color);
    width: 50%;
  }
}

\table(class="center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Architecture}
    \th{Instruction}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{x86_64}
    \td{SQRTSD}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{ARM}
    \td{FSQRT}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{RISC-V}
    \td{FSQRT.S}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{WASM}
    \td{f64.sqrt}
  }
}

\cowel_macro("fno_math_errno"){\ref("https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Optimize-Options.html#index-fno-math-errno"){\tt{-fno-math-errno}}}

Due to this problem,
some compilers provide a flag to disable writing to \tcode{errno} in mathematical functions,
which is \fno_math_errno for GCC and Clang.

\Bex{
Consider the difference in output for the following functions:
\cppblock{
double s(double x) {
  return std::sqrt(x);
}
std::array<double, 4> vs(const std::array<double, 4>& x) {
  return { std::sqrt(x[0]), std::sqrt(x[1]),
           std::sqrt(x[2]), std::sqrt(x[3]) };
}
}

\table(class="center tony-table"){
  \tr{
    \th{\tt{clang++-24 -O3\br-march=x86-64-v3}}
    \th{\tt{clang++-24 -O3\br-march=x86-64-v3 -fno-math-errno}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){s(double):}
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      sqrt@PLT
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        ret
}}
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){s(double):}
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        ret
}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){vs(std::array<double, 4ul> const&):}
        push    r14
        push    rbx
        push    rax
        mov     r14, rsi
        mov     rbx, rdi
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [rsi]
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_2
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 8]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jae     .LBB1_4
.LBB1_5:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        jmp     .LBB1_6
.LBB1_2:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 8]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_5
.LBB1_4:
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
.LBB1_6:
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 8], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 16]
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_8
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 16], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 24]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jae     .LBB1_10
.LBB1_11:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        jmp     .LBB1_12
.LBB1_8:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 16], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 24]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_11
.LBB1_10:
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
.LBB1_12:
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 24], xmm0
        mov     rax, rbx
        add     rsp, 8
        pop     rbx
        pop     r14
        ret
}}
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){vs(std::array<double, 4ul> const&):}
        vsqrtpd ymm0, ymmword ptr [rsi]
        mov     rax, rdi
        vmovupd ymmword ptr [rdi], ymm0
        vzeroupper
        ret
}}
  }
}
Note that while the difference in code size is quite dramatic,
the length of the critical path is not impacted as much.
In both the scalar and vector case,
there is a \q{happy path} (i.e. non-NaN) which is checked for,
and \code("asm"){call sqrt} is only a fallback solution.
}

However, crucially, \fno_math_errno is a non-conforming and non-portable optimization,
as described in the GCC docs:

\Bquote{
\b{\tt{-fno-math-errno}}
\Bindent{\cowel_paragraphs{
Do not set \tt{errno} after calling math functions that are executed with a single instruction,
e.g., \tcode{sqrt}.
A program that relies on IEEE exceptions for math error handling
may want to use this flag for speed while maintaining IEEE arithmetic compatibility.

This option is not turned on by any \tt{-O} option besides \tt{-Ofast}
since it can result in incorrect output for programs
that depend on an exact implementation of IEEE or ISO rules/specifications for math functions.
It may, however, yield faster code for programs
that do not require the guarantees of these specifications.

The default is \tt{-fmath-errno}.

On Darwin systems, the math library never sets \tcode{errno}.
There is therefore no reason for the compiler to consider the possibility that it might,
and \tt{-fno-math-errno} is the default.
}}
}

\h3{\tcode{constexpr} problem}

Since there can be no writes to \tcode{errno} during constant evaluation,
the committee is stuck with two bad options
regarding the use of math functions in constant expressions:
\ul{
  \li{
    Allow \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} to be a constant expression
    and \q{silently} ignore \tcode{errno},
    even if the same expression evaluated at runtime would have modified \tcode{errno}.
  }
  \li{
    Disallow \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} during constant evaluation.
    This is the status quo,
    and prohibits many uses of math functions.
    For example, \tcode{std::ldexp(1, x)} cannot be used
    to check whether \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mi{x}}} is representable as \tcode{double}
    (e.g. inside \tcode{if constexpr})
    because the answer is either \q{yes} or \q{ill-formed};
    at runtime, one can check whether the result is infinity.
  }
}

Optimization flags like \fno_math_errno cannot fix this design problem either.

However, if the standard provided a \q{mode} where math functions do not write to \tcode{errno}
(neither at runtime nor during constant evaluation),
then math functions could always be used in constant expressions
(at least for ISO/IEC 60559 floating-point types)
and would do the same as at runtime
(except for slight precision/rounding differences).
This would still present a portability concern
in the sense that some code would only compile in \q{errno-less mode},
but that is arguably an improvement over the current situation.

\h2{Design}

To address the problems described above,
we propose an \q{errno-less mode} for math functions.
This essentially requires the compiler and standard library to provide a flag
which deactivates writes to \tcode{errno} for math functions.

One major problem with this idea is that on platforms where writing to \tcode{errno} is enabled,
the implementation needs to provide a second copy of \tt{libc} or at least \tt{libm}
that has been compiled in \q{errno-less mode}.
Every single math function would need to have an \q{errno-less} variant available.
On platforms where \q{errno-less mode} is already the default (e.g. Darwin),
no action is required.

The aforementioned \ref("#constexpr-problem") is not resolved in this paper,
but provides motivation for the direction.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\h3(show_number=false){[intro.compliance.general]}

Immediately following \eelis{intro.compliance.general#8}
(i.e. following the definition of \q{hardened implementation}),
insert a new paragraph:

\Bins{
An implementation shall provide a configuration option so that
\tcode{math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO} equals \tcode{0}\iref{cmath.syn}.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[cmath.syn]}

Append a new paragraph to \eelis{cmath.syn}:

\Bins{
The meaning of the macro \tcode{math_errhandling} is the same
as of that in the C standard library header \header{math.h},
except that \tcode{math_errhandling} may equal \tcode{0} even for a hosted implementation.
}

